   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine rend="printed">
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_29">27, RUE CASIMIR-PÉRIER (VII<hi rend="superscript">
                                        <hi rend="underline">E</hi>
                                    </hi>)</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine rend="printed">INVALIDES 70-04</addrLine> </address> </ab>
                </div>  <div type="address">
                    <dateline>Paris	<date when="1936-08-06">August the 6-th. 1936</date> </dateline>
                    <ab> <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="person" key="daws07">The R. H. The Viscount Carlow</name> </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>34 Chesham Place </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>London S. W. </addrLine>
                        </address> </ab> </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <opener>
                        <salute>Dear Lord Carlow</salute>, </opener> <p>I am sending you enclosed <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">the two lettrines</seg> of <name type="person" key="joyc07">Miss Lucia Joyce</name> destined for the illumination of the first letter of the text and of the first letter of the notes for Mr Joyce’s fragment which you intend to publish. Under separate cover I am sending you the text of the fragment in Transition with corrections. </p> <p>I need not impress you with the importance Mr Joyce attaches to the lettrines and would simply ask you to be very careful with the keeping of them. Had it not been for their possible publication I do not think that Mr Joyce would have even considered the issue of a fragment which is constituted of two short parts really hardly destined for separate publication. </p> <p>The price of the reproduction of the lettrines as given me by <name type="person" key="kaha87">Mr J. Kahane</name> from The Obelisk Press, 338 rue Saint-Honoré, Paris, is for 150 copies Frcs. 900.-. </p> <p>You will see that the printing of the fragment presents considerable difficulties from the point of view of typography. The “captions” on the left margin should be <choice>
                            <sic>alligned</sic>
                            <corr>aligned</corr>
                        </choice> with certain words of the text. They are not quite right in the Transition text and I have therefore underlined the word to which the caption refers and hope you will be able to find them. The large type captions on the right are aligned with the beginning of each paragraph. </p> <p>Two corrections will be sent to you subsequently: </p>
                    <list rend="numbered">
                        <item n="1)">A caption on the right margin for the second paragraph. </item>
                        <item n="2)">An addition to the caption on the left margin of page 127 reading “Pompeius Magnus”. </item> </list>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>sincerely yours </salute>
                        <signed> 			<name type="person" key="leon93">Paul Léon</name>
                        </signed> </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">Léon is sending the lettrines A and R to be included in <name type="work">Storiella As She Is Syung</name>. In the printed edition, lettrine A appears at the opening of the main text in “As we there”. Lettrine R, however, intended for the footnote beginning “Rawmeash”, was not used. See reproduction in section for “Images”.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
   </text>
